  "conversationGuide": "Conversation Guide",
  "conversationGuideStep0Question1": "What is this conversation about?",
  "conversationGuideStep0Question1Answer": "The aim of this conversation is to jointly come up with a weekly training plan for you, that you can continue with on your own after you have completed your cardiac rehabilitation programme.",
  "conversationGuideStep0Question1AnswerHealthyLifeStyle": "The aim of this conversation is to jointly come up with a weekly physical activity training plan that you can continue with on your own.",
  "conversationGuideStep0Question2": "Why should you be involved in the decision-making process?",
  "conversationGuideStep0Question2Answer": "There are different ways to design such a training plan, and it is important to include your preferences in the decision.",
  "conversationGuideStep1": "Discuss state of knowledge\nand inform",
  "conversationGuideStep1Headline": "What do you already know about your cardiovascular condition?",
  "conversationGuideStep1Short": "Knowledge",
  "conversationGuideStep1Question1": "What is a cardiovascular condition? / Are you familiar with your cardiovascular condition?",
  "conversationGuideStep1Question1Answer": "<b>Cardiovascular conditions are diseases of the heart and blood vessels.</b>\nThere are many different cardiovascular conditions, but a few account for the majority.\nThe most common cardiovascular conditions are:\n\n• High blood pressure\n• Atherosclerosis (\"calcification\" of the arteries)\n• Coronary heart disease\n• Chronic heart failure\n\nCardiovascular conditions are the most common cause of death in Austria.\n<b>The most serious consequences of cardiovascular conditions include:</b>\n\n• Heart attack (caused by occlusion of the coronary arteries)\n• Stroke (caused by occusion of the arteries in the brain)\n• Cardiac arrhythmias",
  "conversationGuideStep1Question2": "What causes cardiovascular disease? / What are the risk factors for cardiovascular disease?",
  "conversationGuideStep1Question2Answer": "Some cardiovascular conditions have specific and individual causes.\nHowever, in general, it can be said <b>that certain unhealthy behaviours promote cardiovascular disease:</b>\n\n• A diet that is very high in calories, sugar and fat\n• Physical inactivity\n• Alcohol consumption\n• Nicotine use",
  "conversationGuideStep1Question3": "Can cardiovascular conditions be prevented?",
  "conversationGuideStep1Question3Answer": "Cardiovascular conditions can be prevented by making heart-healthy lifestyle choices.\nPeople who have already had a serious event such as a heart attack can prevent further events.\n<b>The most important preventive measures are:</b>\n\n• Regular heart-healthy physical activity and exercise\n• Non-smoking\n• Normal blood pressure\n• Normal blood glucose level\n• Normal blood cholesterol level (through healthy diet and medication)\n• Taking your cardiovascular medication regularly\n• Normal weight\n• Healthy diet",
  "conversationGuideStep1Question4": "What are the effects of heart-healthy physical activity and exercise?",
  "conversationGuideStep1Question4Answer": "Regular heart-healthy physical activity and exercise is one of the most important preventive measures for people with cardiovascular conditions and <more1>in general for everybody</more1>. <more2>Positive effects</more2> are seen up to the highest age - there is no \"age limit\".\n\nPhysical activity is particularly heart-healthy if you exercise at a moderate level of exertion (intensity). Moderate exertion means that you get a little out of breath and can still chat, but you can no longer sing.\n\nFor people who are completely „inaktiv“, already the change to become „a little active“ brings considerable health benefit. \"A little physical activity\" means, for example, walking briskly for 5 minutes several times a day.\n\nA possible disadvantage of physical activity and exercise is a certain risk of injury. However, this is low if done correctly.",
  "conversationGuideStep1Question4AnswerHealthyLifeStyle": "Regular heart-healthy physical activity and exercise is one of the most important preventive measures <more1>for everybody</more1>. <more2>Positive effects</more2> are seen up to the highest age - there is no \"age limit\".\n\nPhysical activity is particularly heart-healthy if you exercise at a moderate level of exertion (intensity). Moderate exertion means that you get a little out of breath and can still chat, but you can no longer sing.\n\nFor people who are completely „inaktiv“, already the change to become „a little active“ brings considerable health benefit. \"A little physical activity\" means, for example, walking briskly for 5 minutes several times a day.\n\nA possible disadvantage of physical activity and exercise is a certain risk of injury. However, this is low if done correctly.",
  "conversationGuideStep1Question4AnswerMore1": "<b>Adults who exercise regularly:</b>\n\n• are fitter and healthier\n• feel better\n• are less likely to get chronic diseases, e.g. cardiovascular conditions, diabetes or different types of cancer\n• have less anxiety and the brain works better after exercise\n• sleep better\n• do not get tired so quickly in day-to-day life, e.g. when climbing stairs or when carrying something heavy\n• are less likely to get overweight",
  "conversationGuideStep1Question4AnswerMore2": "<b>Positive effects of regular heart-healthy physical activity and exercise for people with cardiovascular conditions:</b>\n\n• Improvement in fat metabolism\n• Reduction in overweight and blood pressure\n• Reduction in inflammation\n• Improvement in insulin sensitivity and thereby treatment and prevention of type 2 diabetes\n• Normalisation of the blood glucose level",
  "conversationGuideStep2": "Asking for preferences\nand offering options",
  "conversationGuideStep2Headline": "What kind of physical activity, exercise or sport do you like?",
  "conversationGuideStep2Short": "Preferences",
  "conversationGuideStep2Question1": "What kind of physical activity, exercise or sport do you currently do, or did you enjoy in the past?",
  "conversationGuideStep2Question1Answer": "The idea is not only to think about classic sports and targeted exercise training, but also about social opportunities (e.g. dance, group hikes) and everyday exercise and activities (e.g. walking the dog, housework, gardening, climbing stairs).\n\n• What kind of physical activity, exercise or sport have you already had positive experiences with?\n• What kind of physical activity, exercise or sport have you had negative experiences with?\n• Are there types of physical activity, exercise or sports that you have no experience with, but that would interest you?\n\n<b>Optional (possibly helpful if the person is rather inactive):</b>\n\n• In your usual daily routine, what activities cause you to sweat or breathe hard?\n• Are you or did you used to be in a sports club?\n• In your school days, what did you enjoy in PE (physical education, sports class)?",
  "conversationGuideStep2Question2": "Did you know that everyday activities also count as heart-healthy exercise?",
  "conversationGuideStep2Question2Answer": "<b>Not only classic sports and targeted exercise training are considered heart-healthy exercise, but also everyday movements and activities contribute to a heart-healthy lifestyle.</b>\n\nExamples include walking the dog, housework, gardening, climbing stairs or going up a hill.\n\nThese activities are particularly heart-healthy when they are moderate or vigorous, i.e. when they make your heart beat faster, they make you breathe harder, make you feel warm and cause you to sweat a little.",
  "conversationGuideStep2Question3": "What are the different training methods and what are their advantages?",
  "conversationGuideStep2Question3Answer": "<b>It is recommended to do both endurance-oriented exercise and muscle-strengthening exercise every week.</b>\n\n<more1>Endurance-oriented exercise</more1> generally benefits more the cardiovascular system (your heart and blood vessels), respiratory system (your lungs) and metabolism.\n\n<more2>Muscle-strengthening exercise</more2> is generally more beneficial for posture, bone density, falls prevention and when lifting and moving heavy objects in everyday life.",
  "conversationGuideStep2Question3AnswerMore1": "<b>Endurance-oriented exercise</b>\n\n• You move the whole body rhythmically over a longer period of time.\n• Examples are brisk walking, running, cycling, dancing, basketball, swimming.\n• The heart beats faster, you have to breathe harder, you feel warm and you start to sweat.\n• For adults with chronic diseases it is recommended to do alltogether 150 minutes (2 ½ hours) of endurance-oriented exercise with moderate exertion (intensity) every week.\n• Moderate exertion (intensity) means that the heart beats faster and you have to breathe a little harder - but not too hard (you should still be able to talk, but no longer be able to sing).",
  "conversationGuideStep2Question3AnswerMore2": "<b>Muscle-strengthening exercise</b>\n\n• You specifically exercise your large muscle groups (leg, hip, chest, back, belly, shoulder and arm muscles).\n• Examples are exercises with weights or elastic bands, squats, push-ups.\n• When training to build up strength (hypertrophy training), choose a weight with which you can manage just about 8 to 12 repetitions.\n• In strength endurance training you do 20 to 40 repetitions.\n• For adults with chronic diseases it is recommended to do muscle-strengthening exercise for all large muscle groups twice a week.",
  "conversationGuideStep2Question4": "How often can you imagine incorporating physical activity and exercise into your daily routine?",
  "conversationGuideStep2Question4Answer": "Both <b>longer and structured exercise sessions</b> (e.g. one hour of group exercise every week, half an hour of jogging twice a week) and <b>shorter exercise sessions built into your daily routine</b> (e.g. walking for 10 minutes every day, climbing the stairs once a day instead of taking the lift) are helpful.\n\n<b>If possible, both should be incorporated into your training plan:</b>\n\n• How often could you imagine doing a longer and structured training session?\n• How often could you imagine doing a shorter exercise session that is built into your daily routine?",
  "conversationGuideStep3": "Set meaningful goals",
  "conversationGuideStep3Short": "Goals",
  "conversationGuideStep3Headline": "As you want to increase your physical activity, let's make this goal a little more concrete.",
  "conversationGuideStep3Question1": "What would you personally hope to gain from regular heart-healthy physical activity?",
  "conversationGuideStep3Question1Answer": "This question is deliberately open-ended in order to hear the person's thoughts.\nThe person's thoughts should be recorded without influencing, filtering or judging.\n<more1>Various statements</more1> can be possible here, for example indicating a health or social motivation.\nIt should also be recorded if a person cannot express a concrete expectation.\n\nThese statements can provide starting points for the formulation of concrete physical activity goals in the next step.",
  "conversationGuideStep3Question1AnswerMore1": "<b>Possible goal statements:</b>\n\n• „That I will not have another heart attack“\n• „That I live long enough and stay fit to dance at my grandchild's wedding“\n• „That it keeps my body weight in the healthy range\"\n• \"That it balances out my unhealthy lifestyle“",
  "conversationGuideStep3Question2": "Let's now try to formulate concrete physical activity goals. Something that you want to work towards and that can be checked off when you have achieved it. Is there something that comes to mind?",
  "conversationGuideStep3Question2Answer": "<b>Collect ideas:</b> „It should be something that presents a bit of a challenge for you, and it should also be meaningful to you personally.“\n\n<b>Concrete example:</b> „Other people have set themselves a physical activity goal of hiking a certain route or cycling a certain distance.“\n\n<b>Break down big goals into small steps:</b> „That is a good goal, let's write it down - but it also sounds quite challenging. Shall we formulate an additional goal that represents an interim step on the way there - and what would this interim goal look like?“\n\n<b>Add an \"Achieve by\" date to physical activity goals:</b> \"When would you say you would like to have achieved this goal by?\"\n\nIdeally, physical activity goals should be formulated in such a concrete way that one can clearly judge when a goal has been achieved and it can be <more1>\"ticked off\"</more1>. If a person finds it difficult to think of a concrete \"tickable\" goal, a <more2>vague goal formulation</more2> can also be recorded (e.g. \"move more\" or \"live a little healthier\").",
  "conversationGuideStep3Question2AnswerMore1": "<b>„Formulate a \"tickable\" goal</b>\n\nTypically, so-called <b>\"performance goals\"</b> are formulated. This means that the goal is reached when a certain new physical performance has been achieved, for example \"walking 10,000 steps without a break\".\n\nBut also so-called <b>\"learning goals\" or \"process goals\"</b> can be formulated.\n\n<b>Learning goals</b> are about <b>acquiring new knowledge</b> (e.g. \"Finding a walking path near my home where I can then regularly exercise my walking distance\") or <b>new skills</b> (e.g. \"Learning how to exercise my walking distance on the treadmill at the fitness centre\").\n\n<b>Process goals</b> are about <b>implementing or maintaining existing strategies</b> (e.g. \"always walk at a brisk pace\" or \"continue to walk regularly once a day for 10 minutes\").",
  "conversationGuideStep3Question2AnswerMore2": "<b>Vague formulation of goals</b>\n\nVaguely formulated goals should also be given an \"achieve by\" date.\n\nAt a follow-up meeting, an attempt should then be made again to formulate a concrete \"tickable\" goal. An initial vague goal formulation can be helpful in formulating a concrete goal later on.",
